  "query_preview": "..\n\nSubsidence behind the shortwave this morning dried out the mid \nlevels and the cap has held strong thus far. ACARS data out of DTW \nshows waning CIN however, now around 25 J/kg.  Although the \nlow level wind fields are weak, any localized convergence boundaries \n(lake breeze) could serve as a focal point to support isolated \nthunderstorms. Banking on dry mid levels and lag of well defined \ntrigger to keep coverage limited, as hi res solutions are also \nmuted. Of course, an outflow boundary(s) could trigger \nrenewed activity. In any event, it appears there is a better chance \ntonight toward the southern Michigan border. \n\nFast northwest upper level flow over the Great Lakes region through \nThursday, with embedded impulses/upper level PV filaments tracking \nout of the Northern Plains potentially triggering convection. The \nmain moisture/instability axis is located over the northern Ohio, \nwhich favors the bulk of the storms in that area. However, if upper \nlevel waves are sufficiently strong, the southwest low level \njet will be able to displace activity farther north into southern \nLower Michigan. There are a few models suggesting the I-94 corridor \nto the southern Michigan border will see some shower/thunderstorm \nactivity tonight as K indices peak out around 40 C as 850 \nMB winds increase aoa 30 knots, but remain mostly westerly. The \nsubtle direction shift from due west and exact location of the jet \ncore will make all the difference on location of activity. \nOtherwise, with at least moderate instability/capes (MLcapes  \n2000 J/kg) to tap into along with decent mid level lapse rates, \na marginal risk of severe seems reasonable. \n\nAn even stronger upper level wave is progged to come out of the \nMidwest on Tuesday, supporting higher pops and farther northward \nplacement by Tuesday afternoon. Still, if the system goes upscale \nfaster around Chicago, another dive to the southeast remains \na good possibility, focusing the chances for severe storms and heavy \nrain south of border. Thus, the Ohio Valley has been put into \nenhanced risk in the day 2 SPC outlook. \n\nGradually building 500 MB heights to close our the workweek as \nan upper level ridge axis arrives Friday night (AOA 588 DAM per \neuro ensembles). There ridge is still not strong enough to cap \nactivity, as another strong shortwave and surface reflection \nare progged to move into the Western Great Lakes by Saturday. A risk \nof strong storms and heavy rainfall exists over the weekend. Timing, \nduration, and axis of heavy rainfall remain, however, as UKMET, \nCanadian, and Euro models depict significant differences.",
